// Regression test: a local variable named the same as an imported module
// (here `io`, explicitly imported below) must resolve as the variable, not
// the module, when used as a method-call base -- ordinary lexical
// shadowing, same as everywhere else in the language.
//
// Before the fix this was worse than a wrong diagnostic: Resolver::
// visitMethodCall() checked SymTable_.hasModule() before checking for an
// actual declared variable, so `io.show()` failed name resolution entirely
// ("Unknown function 'io.show'"). Fixing that surfaced a SECOND, independent
// copy of the same bug in Codegen::MethodCallExprAST::codegen(), which
// re-derived "is this a module call" from the raw variable name via
// GlobalSymbolTable->HasModule() instead of trusting the Resolver's verdict
// -- causing a hard compiler crash (out-of-bounds std::vector access,
// ArgVals[Idx] against the wrong function's argument count) once the
// Resolver started correctly treating `io` as a struct-typed variable.
// Both fixes are required together for this file to pass.

import io;

struct Thing {
    val: int
}

// `io` here is the module -- this function has no local variable named
// `io`, so this exercises the genuine (non-shadowed) module-call path in
// the same file as the shadowed case below.
fn Thing.show(): void {
    io.printline(self.val);
}

fn main(): int {
    // Inside this scope, `io` is a Thing variable, not the module -- every
    // reference to `io` below must resolve as the variable.
    mut io: Thing = Thing { val: 42 };
    io.show();
    assert(io.val == 42, "");

    return 0;
}
